const o={toc:[{id:"undertone-matters-more-than-skin-depth",text:"Undertone Matters More Than Skin Depth",level:2},{id:"how-do-you-find-your-undertone",text:"How Do You Find Your Undertone?",level:2},{id:"the-vein-test",text:"The vein test",level:3},{id:"the-jewellery-test",text:"The jewellery test",level:3},{id:"the-white-cloth-test",text:"The white cloth test",level:3},{id:"hair-colour-for-warm-undertones",text:"Hair Colour for Warm Undertones",level:2},{id:"hair-colour-for-cool-undertones",text:"Hair Colour for Cool Undertones",level:2},{id:"hair-colour-for-neutral-undertones",text:"Hair Colour for Neutral Undertones",level:2},{id:"quick-guide-hair-colour-for-indian-skin-by-undertone",text:"Quick Guide: Hair Colour for Indian Skin by Undertone",level:2},{id:"working-with-a-dark-indian-base",text:"Working With a Dark Indian Base",level:2},{id:"popular-looks-on-indian-hair",text:"Popular Looks on Indian Hair",level:2},{id:"covering-greys-with-the-right-shade",text:"Covering Greys With the Right Shade",level:2},{id:"think-about-maintenance-too",text:"Think About Maintenance Too",level:2},{id:"what-happens-at-your-colour-consultation",text:"What Happens at Your Colour Consultation?",level:2},{id:"bring-the-right-inspiration-photos",text:"Bring the right inspiration photos",level:3},{id:"how-do-you-look-after-your-new-colour",text:"How Do You Look After Your New Colour?",level:2},{id:"a-quick-checklist-before-you-colour",text:"A Quick Checklist Before You Colour",level:2},{id:"choose-your-shade-in-bangalore",text:"Choose Your Shade in Bangalore",level:2}],before:`<p>The right hair colour can brighten your complexion and make your features stand out. The wrong one can make skin look dull or tired, even if the shade looked lovely on someone else. Choosing a <strong>hair colour for Indian skin</strong> isn&#39;t about following trends. It&#39;s about understanding your undertone and picking a shade family that works with it.</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<p>This guide explains how to find your undertone, which shades tend to flatter warm, cool and neutral skin, how your natural base affects the result, and what to discuss at your colour consultation.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="undertone-matters-more-than-skin-depth">Undertone Matters More Than Skin Depth</h2>
<p>Indian skin covers a wide range, from fair to deep. But when it comes to hair colour, your <strong>undertone</strong> matters more than how light or dark your skin is.</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<p>Your undertone is the subtle colour beneath the surface of your skin. It&#39;s usually one of three:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Warm:</strong> golden, peachy or yellow hints</li>
<li><strong>Cool:</strong> pink, red or bluish hints</li>
<li><strong>Neutral:</strong> a balance of both</li>
</ul>
<p>Two people with the same skin depth can have different undertones, and the same shade can look completely different on each of them.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="how-do-you-find-your-undertone">How Do You Find Your Undertone?</h2>
<p>Try these simple checks in natural daylight:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<h3 id="the-vein-test">The vein test</h3>
<p>Look at the veins on the inside of your wrist.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Greenish veins</strong> usually suggest a warm undertone</li>
<li><strong>Bluish or purple veins</strong> usually suggest a cool undertone</li>
<li><strong>A mix</strong>, or hard to tell, often means neutral</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="the-jewellery-test">The jewellery test</h3>
<ul>
<li>If <strong>gold</strong> jewellery makes your skin look brighter, you&#39;re probably warm</li>
<li>If <strong>silver</strong> looks better, you&#39;re probably cool</li>
<li>If both look good, you may be neutral</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="the-white-cloth-test">The white cloth test</h3>
<p>Hold a pure white cloth near your face. If your skin looks golden or yellowish against it, you&#39;re likely warm. If it looks pinkish, you&#39;re likely cool.</p>
<p>These are guides, not rules. Your stylist will look at your skin, eyes and natural hair together.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="hair-colour-for-warm-undertones">Hair Colour for Warm Undertones</h2>
<p>Warm undertones are very common in Indian skin. They often glow with shades that have golden or red warmth:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>Caramel</strong></li>
<li><strong>Honey</strong></li>
<li><strong>Copper</strong></li>
<li><strong>Golden brown</strong></li>
<li><strong>Warm chocolate</strong></li>
<li><strong>Auburn</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>These shades echo the golden tones in your skin and tend to make your complexion look brighter and healthier. Caramel and honey balayage on a dark base is a particularly popular, natural-looking choice.</p>
<p><strong>Shades to be careful with:</strong> very ashy or icy tones can sometimes make warm skin look dull or washed out.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="hair-colour-for-cool-undertones">Hair Colour for Cool Undertones</h2>
<p>Cool undertones often suit shades without too much golden warmth:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ash brown</strong></li>
<li><strong>Mocha</strong></li>
<li><strong>Cool dark brown</strong></li>
<li><strong>Burgundy and wine shades</strong></li>
<li><strong>Plum</strong></li>
</ul>
<p>These shades complement the pink or blue hints in cool skin.</p>
<p><strong>Shades to be careful with:</strong> very brassy, orange or strong golden tones can sometimes clash with cool undertones.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="hair-colour-for-neutral-undertones">Hair Colour for Neutral Undertones</h2>
<p>Neutral undertones have the most flexibility. Many shades work, including soft browns, mocha, soft caramel and gentle balayage. Your eye colour, your natural hair colour and the look you want can guide the final choice.</p>
<h2 id="quick-guide-hair-colour-for-indian-skin-by-undertone">Quick Guide: Hair Colour for Indian Skin by Undertone</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Undertone</th>
<th>Often flattering</th>
<th>Use with care</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Warm</td>
<td>Caramel, honey, copper, golden brown, warm chocolate</td>
<td>Very ashy or icy tones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Cool</td>
<td>Ash brown, mocha, cool dark brown, burgundy, plum</td>
<td>Brassy or strong golden tones</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Neutral</td>
<td>Soft browns, mocha, soft caramel, gentle balayage</td>
<td>Very extreme shades in either direction</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
</div>
`,after:`<h2 id="working-with-a-dark-indian-base">Working With a Dark Indian Base</h2>
<p>Most Indian hair has a dark natural base. That affects which shades are easy to achieve:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>A few shades lighter</strong> than your natural colour usually looks the most natural and grows out softly.</li>
<li><strong>Much lighter shades</strong> usually need pre-lightening on dark hair, which takes more care and maintenance.</li>
<li><strong>Vivid fashion shades</strong> almost always need pre-lightening first. Our guide to fashion and pastel hair colour explains more.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you&#39;re unsure about going much lighter, a softer technique like balayage or a few face-framing highlights can add brightness without a big commitment. Read <a href="/blog/balayage-vs-highlights">balayage vs highlights</a> to compare.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="popular-looks-on-indian-hair">Popular Looks on Indian Hair</h2>
<p>A few looks that work well on a dark Indian base, depending on how much change you want:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>Subtle:</strong> a rich chocolate or warm brown global colour, a shade or two lighter than your natural base.</li>
<li><strong>Soft brightness:</strong> caramel or honey balayage through the mid-lengths and ends.</li>
<li><strong>Face-framing:</strong> a few highlights around the face for brightness where it shows most.</li>
<li><strong>Rich and deep:</strong> burgundy, wine or plum tones, which suit many cool and neutral undertones.</li>
<li><strong>Bold:</strong> fashion shades or streaks, usually with pre-lightening.</li>
</ul>
<p>Your stylist can show you where each look sits on your hair and how it will grow out.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="covering-greys-with-the-right-shade">Covering Greys With the Right Shade</h2>
<p>If you&#39;re covering greys, the shade choice matters even more. Very dark shades can make regrowth show sharply, while a softer brown close to your natural base grows out more gently. Some people add soft highlights to blend greys rather than covering them completely. Our guide to <a href="/blog/root-touch-up-frequency">how often you need a root touch-up</a> explains how to plan maintenance.</p>
<h2 id="think-about-maintenance-too">Think About Maintenance Too</h2>
<p>Your skin tone isn&#39;t the only factor. Consider:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li><strong>How far from your natural shade you&#39;re going.</strong> The bigger the difference, the more frequent the touch-ups.</li>
<li><strong>Greys.</strong> If you&#39;re covering greys, a shade close to your natural base grows out more softly.</li>
<li><strong>Your lifestyle.</strong> If you can&#39;t visit often, a blended, low-maintenance technique like balayage may suit you better than bold global colour.</li>
</ul>
</details>
<h2 id="what-happens-at-your-colour-consultation">What Happens at Your Colour Consultation?</h2>
<p>At Xing Premium Unisex Salon, every <a href="/services/global-hair-color">hair colour service</a> starts with a consultation. Your stylist considers:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ol>
<li><strong>Your skin tone and undertone</strong></li>
<li><strong>Your natural base colour</strong></li>
<li><strong>Your colour history</strong>, including any previous colour or treatments</li>
<li><strong>The result you want</strong>, using your inspiration photos</li>
</ol>
<p>They then plan the shade and technique, including whether pre-lightening is needed. If you&#39;re unsure about a shade, a consultation with a strand test is the surest way to preview the result. If it&#39;s your first time colouring or you have sensitive skin, ask about a patch test before your appointment.</p>
<h3 id="bring-the-right-inspiration-photos">Bring the right inspiration photos</h3>
<p>Look for photos of people with a similar skin tone and natural hair colour to yours. A shade on very fair skin with naturally light hair may look quite different on a dark Indian base.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="how-do-you-look-after-your-new-colour">How Do You Look After Your New Colour?</h2>
<p>Once you&#39;ve found your shade, keep it looking its best:</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li>Use a colour-safe, sulphate-free shampoo</li>
<li>Wash with cool or lukewarm water</li>
<li>Protect your hair from strong sun</li>
<li>Book a hair spa or add Olaplex to keep coloured hair healthy</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide on <a href="/blog/make-hair-colour-last">how to make hair colour last longer</a> has more tips.</p>
</details>
<h2 id="a-quick-checklist-before-you-colour">A Quick Checklist Before You Colour</h2>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<ul>
<li>Check your undertone in daylight</li>
<li>Choose two or three inspiration photos on a similar skin tone and base</li>
<li>Decide how much maintenance you&#39;re happy with</li>
<li>Ask about a strand test if you&#39;re unsure, and a patch test if it&#39;s your first time</li>
<li>Plan your colour-safe routine at home</li>
</ul>
</details>
<h2 id="choose-your-shade-in-bangalore">Choose Your Shade in Bangalore</h2>
<p>The best hair colour for Indian skin is one that works with your undertone, suits your natural base and fits how much maintenance you want. Warm undertones often glow with caramel and honey; cool undertones often suit ash and mocha; neutral undertones can wear many shades.</p>
<details class="read-more"><summary>Read more</summary>
<p>Send your inspiration photos on WhatsApp and book a colour consultation at Xing Premium Unisex Salon in Sarjapur Road, HSR Layout or Brookfield.</p>
</details>
`,takeaways:["Your undertone (warm, cool or neutral) matters more than how light or dark your skin is.","Warm undertones often glow with caramel, honey, copper and golden browns.","Cool undertones often suit ash, mocha, cool browns and burgundy.","On a dark Indian base, softer shades a few levels lighter usually look most natural.","A consultation, and a strand test if you're unsure, is the surest way to preview the result."],faqs:[{q:"Which hair colour suits Indian skin?",a:"It depends on your undertone more than your skin depth. Warm undertones often suit caramel, honey, copper and golden browns. Cool undertones often suit ash, mocha and cool browns. Your stylist will help you choose."},{q:"How do I know if my undertone is warm or cool?",a:"Look at the veins on your wrist in daylight: greenish suggests warm, bluish suggests cool, and a mix suggests neutral. Gold jewellery flattering you suggests warm; silver suggests cool."},{q:"Can I go blonde with Indian skin?",a:"Lighter shades can look beautiful, but they usually need pre-lightening on a dark base, and the right tone matters. Softer golden or caramel blondes often look more natural. Your stylist will advise you."},{q:"Should I do a strand test before colouring?",a:"If you're unsure about a shade, a consultation with a strand test is the surest way to preview the result. If it's your first time colouring or you have sensitive skin, ask about a patch test too."}]};export{o as default};
